src/bin/pgbench/README

pgbench Architecture and Developer Guide
========================================

Overview
--------
pgbench is PostgreSQL's built-in benchmarking driver.  Its source code is
organized into modular subsystems with clear ownership boundaries:

  - pgbench.c / pgbench.h
      CLI argument parsing, global configuration, and top-level orchestration.
  - stats.c / stats.h
      Statistical accumulators (SimpleStats, StatsData), latency tracking,
      and interval/summary reporting primitives.
  - variable.c / variable.h
      Scoped variable store (Variables, VariableScope, Variable) and typed
      runtime value system (PgBenchValue).
  - poller.c / poller.h
      Cross-platform non-blocking socket multiplexing (ppoll() or select()).
  - script.c / script.h, exprscan.l, exprparse.y
      Custom and built-in script parsing, flex/bison expression compilation,
      and runtime expression evaluation (evaluateExpr()).
  - commands.c / commands.h
      Pluggable backslash meta-command handler registry (CommandDescriptor)
      and execution routines.
  - init.c / init.h
      Database schema initialization (-i / --init-steps) and data population.
  - engine.c / engine.h
      Non-blocking client state machine (advanceConnectionState()), worker
      thread runner (threadRun()), and result/log output.
  - src/common/pgbench_funcs.c / src/include/common/pgbench_funcs.h
      Frontend/backend-agnostic random distributions, hashes, permutations,
      and data generators shared between pgbench and contrib/pgbench.

Value Type System (variable.c / variable.h)
-------------------------------------------
Runtime values in expressions and variables are represented by PgBenchValue,
a tagged union supporting:

  - PGBT_NO_VALUE : unparsed raw string in Variable.svalue
  - PGBT_NULL     : SQL/expression NULL
  - PGBT_INT      : 64-bit signed integer (int64)
  - PGBT_DOUBLE   : IEEE 754 double precision float (double)
  - PGBT_BOOLEAN  : boolean (bool)
  - PGBT_TEXT     : heap-allocated NUL-terminated string (char *)
  - PGBT_TIMESTAMP: microseconds since 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC (int64)
  - PGBT_ARRAY    : 1D dynamic array of PgBenchValue (PgBenchArray)

Memory Ownership Invariants:
  - PGBT_TEXT (u.sval) and PGBT_ARRAY (u.aval.elems) own heap allocations.
  - Always use copyValue(dest, src) to duplicate a PgBenchValue and
    freeValue(pv) before overwriting or discarding an owned PgBenchValue.
  - Variable.svalue caches the formatted string representation for SQL
    interpolation (:var and :var[idx]) and is invalidated whenever the
    underlying Variable.value is updated.

Shared Benchmark Functions (src/common/pgbench_funcs.c)
-------------------------------------------------------
Low-level mathematical, statistical, string, and calendar functions live in
src/common/pgbench_funcs.c (declared in src/include/common/pgbench_funcs.h)
so they can be linked into both the frontend pgbench binary and the backend
contrib/pgbench extension:

  - Random distributions: pgbench_random(), pgbench_random_gaussian(),
    pgbench_random_exponential(), pgbench_random_zipfian(),
    pgbench_random_poisson()
  - Hashes & permutations: pgbench_hash_fnv1a(), pgbench_hash_murmur2(),
    pgbench_permute()
  - TPC-C generators: pgbench_nurand(), pgbench_c_last(),
    pgbench_random_astring(), pgbench_random_nstring()
  - Calendar & timestamp arithmetic: pgbench_parse_timestamp(),
    pgbench_format_timestamp(), pgbench_date_add(),
    pgbench_int_to_date_window()

All PRNG-dependent routines take an explicit `pg_prng_state *state` pointer.
In src/bin/pgbench, each client passes `&st->cs_func_rs`; in contrib/pgbench,
the backend passes its session-local `&pgbench_ext_prng_state`.

Script Control Flow & Structured Exception Handling (script.c, commands.c, engine.c)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
During script parsing (`parseScript()` in `script.c`), a static linking pass
validates and resolves jump targets (`jump_target` and `endtry_target` on
`Command` structs) for all block-structured meta-commands:

  - `or var start .. end [BY s]` ... `\endfor`:
      `META_FOR.jump_target` points to `META_ENDFOR + 1`;
      `META_ENDFOR.jump_target` points back to `META_FOR`.
      Runtime state is tracked on `st->loop_stack[MAX_LOOP_DEPTH]`.
  - `\while expr` ... `\endwhile`:
      `META_WHILE.jump_target` points to `META_ENDWHILE + 1`;
      `META_ENDWHILE.jump_target` points back to `META_WHILE`.
  - `	ry` ... `[\catch ['SQLSTATE']]` ... `\endtry`:
      `META_TRY.jump_target` points to `META_CATCH` (or `META_ENDTRY` if no
      `\catch` is present); `META_TRY.endtry_target` and `META_CATCH.jump_target`
      point to `META_ENDTRY`.
      Runtime state is tracked on `st->try_stack[MAX_TRY_DEPTH]`, saving the
      enclosing `cstack_depth` and `loop_depth`.

When `readCommandResponse()` in `engine.c` encounters `PGRES_FATAL_ERROR`, it
extracts `PQresultErrorField(res, PG_DIAG_SQLSTATE)` and checks:
  1. `st->expect_error` (set by `\expect_error ['SQLSTATE']`), or
  2. The innermost active `st->try_stack` frame not yet in its `\catch` block.
If matched, `:sqlstate` and `:error_message` are stored in `st->variables`,
inner conditional/loop frames are unwound, `expected_rollbacks` is incremented,
and `CSTATE_END_COMMAND` transitions directly to `caught_jump_target`.

Result Set Inspection & Array Binding (commands.c, variable.c)
--------------------------------------------------------------
- `processGSetResult()` inspects each column value returned by `\gset` or
  `set`.  When a value starts with `{` and ends with `}`,
  `parseArrayLiteral()` parses the 1D PostgreSQL array representation into a
  `PGBT_ARRAY` value (coercing unquoted elements to `NULL`, `int64`, `double`,
  `bool`, or `text`) and binds `<prefix><colname>_count` to the array length.
- `processGSetMetaResult()` / `populateGSetMeta()` record result metadata
  (`_rowcount`, `_ntuples`, `_nfields`, `_sqlstate`, `_status`, `_elapsed_us`)
  for both trailing `\gset_meta` and standalone `\gset_meta` commands.

Pacing: Dynamic \sleep, \keying_time, and \think_time (script.c, commands.c, engine.c)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`parseSleepCommand()` parses the optional trailing unit (`us`, `ms`, `s`) and
compiles the preceding tokens into `cmd->expr` using the full expression parser.
At runtime, `handle_cmd_sleep()` evaluates `cmd->expr` (supporting both integer
and floating-point results), scales by the unit multiplier into microseconds,
records `st->sleep_begin` and `st->sleep_type`, and transitions the client to
`CSTATE_SLEEP` (monitored non-blockingly by `poller.c`).
When `CSTATE_SLEEP` finishes, `META_KEYING_TIME` and `META_THINK_TIME`
accumulate elapsed microseconds into `st->txn_keying_time` and
`st->txn_think_time`, allowing `StatsData` to report both total cycle latency
and database `response time average` (latency minus keying and think time).

Heterogeneous Client Groups & Sequential Streams (pgbench.c, engine.c)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
`parseClientGroup()` parses repeatable `--group` specifications into
`client_groups[MAX_GROUPS]`.  When groups are active:
  - Total `nclients` and `nthreads` are the sums of per-group `nclients` and
    `nthreads`.
  - Each `TState` and `CState` is assigned a `group_id` and a 0-based
    group-local `stream_id` (exposed in client variables `:group_name` and
    `:stream_id`).
  - `chooseScriptForClient()` restricts script selection to the group's
    `script_indices[]`.  When `sequential=true`, scripts are chosen via
    `pgbench_permute(st->seq_counter++, grp->num_scripts, st->stream_id + 1)`.

HDR Latency Histogram, SLA Validation & JSON Reporting (stats.c, engine.c)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
- `StatsData` embeds a 3,968-bucket sub-bucketed base-2 HDR latency histogram
  (`HDR_SUB_BITS = 7`, 128 sub-buckets per power-of-2 octave across 31 octaves).
  Recording a sample (`accumStats()`) uses `pg_leftmost_one_pos64()` in O(1)
  time with zero heap allocations, and `mergeStats()` sums bucket counts across
  threads, client groups, and scripts.
- `getLatencyPercentile()` interpolates within the target sub-bucket to bounded
  ~0.78% relative error for `p50`, `p90`, `p95`, `p99`, `p99.9`, or arbitrary
  `--sla` percentiles.
- `printResults()` evaluates all `sla_rules[]`, derives `tpmC` and `QphH@SF`,
  and optionally writes the structured JSON report via `writeJsonReport()`.

How to Add a New Built-in Function or Meta-Command
--------------------------------------------------
To add a new built-in script function:
  1. If backend-reusable, implement the core routine in
     `src/common/pgbench_funcs.c` and `src/include/common/pgbench_funcs.h`.
  2. Add a `PGBENCH_*` enum value to `PgBenchFunction` in `src/bin/pgbench/pgbench.h`.
  3. Register the function name and arity in `PGBENCH_FUNCTIONS[]` in `script.c`.
  4. Implement its evaluation case in `evalStandardFunc()` in `script.c`.
  5. Document it in `doc/src/sgml/ref/pgbench.sgml` and add TAP tests in
     `src/bin/pgbench/t/001_pgbench_with_server.pl`.

To add a new backslash meta-command:
  1. Add a `META_*` enum value to `MetaCommand` in `src/bin/pgbench/script.h`.
  2. Parse its syntax in `process_backslash_command()` in `script.c` (and link
     any control-flow targets in `parseScript()`).
  3. Implement `handle_cmd_<name>()` and register it in `command_descriptors[]`
     in `src/bin/pgbench/commands.c`.
  4. Document it in `doc/src/sgml/ref/pgbench.sgml` and add tests in `t/`.

Running the Test Suites
-----------------------
- TAP tests for `src/bin/pgbench`:
    make -C src/bin/pgbench check
- SQL regression tests for `contrib/pgbench`:
    make -C contrib/pgbench check
- SGML documentation validation:
    make -C doc/src/sgml check
